PART A – STANDARDS AND SPECIFICATION MANAGEMENT

1. Dynamic Standards Library

Create an internal standards-reference architecture for:

- MoRTH Specifications for Road and Bridge Works.
- Applicable IRC Codes and Special Publications.
- Relevant BIS/IS standards referenced by the applicable specifications.
- Project-specific technical specifications.
- Approved construction drawings.
- Approved Quality Assurance Plan.
- Contract-specific amendments and addenda.

Required standard-management inputs

- Standard organisation.
- Standard/code number.
- Standard title.
- Edition/revision/year.
- Applicable amendment number.
- Section/chapter.
- Clause number.
- Table/figure reference.
- Applicable work item.
- Material category.
- Test requirement.
- Sampling frequency.
- Acceptance criteria.
- Units.
- Source document.
- Effective date.
- Project applicability.
- Approval status.

Critical requirements

1. Never assume that the newest published standard automatically applies to an existing contract.
2. Allow the project engineer to select the contractually applicable edition and amendments.
3. Allow project-specific approved criteria to override generic reference defaults where contractually permitted.
4. Display the exact clause and source for every specification-based compliance result.
5. Show an alert when a reference is missing, outdated or not verified.
6. Never invent a MoRTH clause, IRC code, test frequency, tolerance or acceptance limit.
7. Allow standards to be updated without changing historical inspection results.
8. Preserve the original reference version used when a report was created.

